it's more than two weeks since the first  reports of around 200 chinese vessels  anchored at whitson reef in the south  china sea  many are still there china says there  are fishing boats  sheltering from stormy seas the  philippine government believes  they are manned by a chinese militia and  it wants them to leave  saying the area is within the  philippines economic zone  defense secretary delfin lorenzana  believes china is attempting to occupy  more of the disputed waterway  a charge beijing denies  the philippine side should view this  issue objectively and correctly  immediately stop unwarranted hype up and  avoid negative impacts on bilateral  relations  and overall peace and stability of the  south china sea  beijing continues to assert it owns most  of the south china sea  despite that claim being rejected by an  international tribunal at the hague in  2016.  in manila there is growing anger against  the chinese embassy  after it issued a statement criticizing  the philippines defense secretary's  comments  as unprofessional and perplexing there  have been calls for the chinese  ambassador to be expelled  with embassy officials warned they were  in the country  as guests of the philippine government  the philippine foreign affairs office  said  it will launch diplomatic protests about  the whitson reef occupation  unprecedented so why why the gal to  speak against a member of our cabinet  who is simply defending national  interests  standing up for the territorial  integrity of the philippines  based on international law across asia  other countries are also protesting  china's military expansions  china held naval drills involving an  aircraft carrier battle group near  taiwan  while japan criticized what it called  chinese coast guard's aggressive  behavior  around the senkaku islands meanwhile the  u.s navy carrier theodore roosevelt  and its strike group re-entered the  south china sea over the weekend  its second appearance this year  china says this is highly provocative  but the u.s navy says  it has a right to operate in the south  china sea  underlining the importance of freedom of  navigation  in international waters in the  philippines though much of the blame is  still directed  towards the government people here say  the territories under the control of the  philippines in the south china sea  have never been more threatened and that  president rodrigo duterte's so-called  pivot to china  in 2016 is turning out to be  a capitulation jamella allendogen  al jazeera manila
 

